            <ArticleTitle>Renal hematoma: an unusual cause of acute abdomen and fetal demise in severe pre-eclampsia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>456-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/0019-5359.109270</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Retroperitoneal hematoma occurs rarely in an obstetric patient. Renal hematoma may present with signs and symptoms, which may mimic the clinical presentation of abruptio placentae or rupture uterus. Although renal hematoma is sometimes a surgical emergency due to hypovolemic shock, conservative management by angiographic embolization may be done. Timely diagnosis and management is required to decrease the maternal mortality and morbidity. We hereby report a case of spontaneous renal hematoma in a patient with severe pre-eclampsia who presented with acute abdomen and intrauterine fetal death.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
